module Round
    intent =
        "Rounding module of the K5 division proof: the paper's three rounding"
        "procedures trunc, away and sticky (Section 5.2, p.13) on the Stage-1"
        "normalized representation. The paper defines each as an operation on the"
        "significand, signed and scaled: round(x,n) = sigma_x * roundn(s_x,n) *"
        "2^(e_x), where the significand truncation is truncn(s,n) = floor(s*2^(n-1))"
        "/ 2^(n-1) (Section 5.1, p.11). On the normalized model a value is sigma *"
        "sigBits * 2^exp / 2^(width-1) with s_x = sigBits / 2^(width-1) in [1,2), so"
        "the truncated significand INTEGER (the n-bit significand scaled by 2^(n-1))"
        "is floor(sigBits * 2^(n-1) / 2^(width-1)) -- a single Euclidean floor"
        "division. trunc keeps the sign and the stored exponent and sets the width"
        "to n; using the STORED exponent sidesteps the recursive binary-exponent"
        "function, and is exactly faithful because the model is already normalized"
        "(s_x in [1,2))."
        "away(x,n) (Section 5.2, p.13) rounds the significand AWAY from zero:"
        "awayn(s,n) = s if truncn(s,n) = s, else truncn(s,n) + 2^(1-n) -- truncn"
        "rounded up. Its integer significand is awaySig(f,n) (= awayn*2^(n-1), the"
        "ceiling of sigBits*2^(n-1) / 2^(width-1)); when rounding up carries the"
        "significand to 2 the result is renormalized value-preservingly (exponent"
        "+1), exactly like fpMul, so the stored exponent stays the true exponent."
        "sticky(x,n) is modelled in the FAITHFUL round-to-odd (RTL/rto) TRUNCATING"
        "form of the ACL2 RTL lineage (rel1 lib1/round.lisp `defun sticky`, Russinoff"
        "AMD 1996-98; renamed `rto` in rel11) -- NOT the draft paper's printed stickyn"
        "branch, which is a known transcription typo (it returns x unchanged when the"
        "kept bit is already 1, falsifying Lemma 7.3.2 Sticky Plus on ~17% of cases)."
        "RTL sticky truncates to n-1 bits then forces the n-th bit to 1, which keeps"
        "the significand strictly below 2 -- sticky never carries."
        "Landed universal on the Lean kernel: Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19) -- trunc, away and"
        "sticky are all sign-symmetric, round(-x,i) = -round(x,i) ('Analogous lemmas"
        "hold for away and sticky'); Lemma 7.2.6 (p.19) -- trunc keeps the exponent of"
        "a non-zero x; Lemma 7.2.7 (p.19) -- e(away(x,i)) is e_x or 1+e_x (the"
        "[e_x, e_x+1] range); Lemma 7.2.8 (p.19) -- e(sticky(x,i)) = e_x, sticky keeps"
        "the exponent; and the rounding-COMPOSITION lemmas Lemma 7.2.10 (p.20) --"
        "trunc(trunc(x,i),j) = trunc(x,i) for i <= j -- and Lemma 7.2.11 (p.20) --"
        "trunc(away(x,i),j) = away(x,i) for i <= j (re-truncating an already-i-bit"
        "value to the wider j >= i is the identity). Both reduce after"
        "cross-multiplication to one EXACT-DIVISION CANCEL on the outer truncation's"
        "floor: floor(s*2^b / 2^a) * 2^a = s*2^b when 0 <= a <= b, valid because"
        "2^a | 2^b (the power-of-two homomorphism). That cancel is itself a generic"
        "Aver law on floorDiv (dividesPow2Multiple, universal); the composition"
        "lemmas CITE it -- no per-figure proof. The RTL trunc-sticky composition"
        "(Lemma 7.3.2's foundational rung, trunc(sticky(x,n),m) = trunc(x,m) for"
        "m < n) also landed universal: its significand identity coarsens away a"
        "DOUBLED round-to-odd low bit, closed by the generic floor-composition rung"
        "-- the same shape-only floor arithmetic (cancel-common-factor, absorb-"
        "remainder, nested-floor-collapse) plus the power-of-two homomorphism, with"
        "grind composing the exponent regrouping, again no per-figure proof."
        "Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19) and Lemma 7.2.4"
        "(p.19), the RECONSTRUCTION halves -- away(x,i) = x + epsilon and sticky(x,i) ="
        "x + epsilon, with epsilon = round(x,i) - x -- both landed universal (the"
        "additive complement of Lemma 7.2.2's trunc(x,i) = x - epsilon; the exact"
        "rational ring identity x + (round(x,i) - x) = round(x,i), closed by the"
        "equational keystone case-splitting the rounding match). Deferred (kwiatki):"
        "Lemma 7.2.3/7.2.4's STRICT BOUND |epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1) and Lemma 7.2.3's"
        "SAME-SIGN condition (both stated EXACTLY as the paper and sample-verified, NOT"
        "yet universal): the away error is the COMPLEMENT |W - r| of the truncation"
        "remainder and the sticky error is a round-to-odd half-step, but a faithful"
        "fpAway/fpSticky is PIECEWISE (awaySig's exact/round-up split, the carry"
        "renormalization Lemma 7.2.7 needs, sticky's three branches), so the trunc bound"
        "arm -- which reads a SINGLE floor quotient off one clean record -- needs a"
        "genuinely new matched-rounded-value case-split before it can fire."
    depends [Domain.Fprep, Domain.Rational]
    exposes [floorDiv, floorDivWindow, truncFitsWindow, fpTrunc, fpNegate, truncNegateSymmetric, truncKeepsExponent, truncError, truncErrorMagnitude, truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg, fpValueMagnitude, fpValueMagnitudePos, truncErrorReconstructs, truncErrorBound, truncErrorSameSign, truncComposes, awaySig, fpAway, awayNegateSymmetric, awayExpInRange, awayTruncComposes, truncStickyComposes, awayError, awayErrorReconstructs, awayErrorBound, awayErrorSameSign, stickyHalf, fpSticky, stickyNegateSymmetric, stickyKeepsExponent, stickyError, stickyErrorReconstructs, stickyErrorBound]
    effects []

fn floorDiv(a: Int, d: Int) -> Int
    ? "Euclidean floor division a / d (the floor of the exact quotient for d > 0); guarded to 0 at d = 0. The divisor here is always a positive power of two, so it is the exact floor."
    Result.withDefault(Int.div(a, d), 0)

verify floorDiv
    floorDiv(7, 2) => 3
    floorDiv(52, 8) => 6
    floorDiv(96, 8) => 12

// The exact-division cancel over a power-of-two divisor that manifestly
// divides the dividend: flooring s * 2^b by 2^a and scaling back by 2^a
// recovers s * 2^b exactly, floor(s * 2^b / 2^a) * 2^a = s * 2^b, when
// 0 <= a <= b. The divisor 2^a divides s * 2^b because 2^a | 2^b (the
// power-of-two homomorphism 2^b = 2^a * 2^(b-a)), so the Euclidean floor
// loses nothing. Generic over the integer s and the two exponents -- the
// exact-division cancel for ANY provably-dividing floorDiv. This is the
// integer engine the rounding-composition lemmas 7.2.10 / 7.2.11 reduce to
// after cross-multiplication: their outer truncation floor-divides an
// already-fitting significand by a power of two it is a multiple of, then the
// other side scales by that same power.

verify floorDiv law dividesPow2Multiple
    given s: Int = [13, 3, 1]
    given a: Int = [0, 1, 2]
    given b: Int = [2, 3, 4]
    when 0 <= a
    when a <= b
    floorDiv(s * Domain.Fprep.pow2(b), Domain.Fprep.pow2(a)) * Domain.Fprep.pow2(a) => s * Domain.Fprep.pow2(b)

// The nested Euclidean floor collapses to a single floor by the product of the
// divisors: floor(floor(a, d), e) = floor(a, d * e) for positive d, e. The
// classic Euclidean nested-floor identity -- flooring by d, then by e, is
// flooring once by d * e -- proven UNIVERSALLY in PURE CORE (no Mathlib) by the
// core lemma Int.ediv_ediv_of_nonneg. Generic over the dividend and BOTH
// divisors (any positive divisors, not just powers of two), the content-blind
// nested-floor rung: the same rung closes ANY nested Euclidean floor collapse.

verify floorDiv law nestedFloorCollapse
    given a: Int = [52, 96, 7, 0 - 13]
    given d: Int = [2, 3, 8]
    given e: Int = [2, 5]
    when 0 < d
    when 0 < e
    floorDiv(floorDiv(a, d), e) => floorDiv(a, d * e)

fn floorDivWindow(a: Int, k: Int) -> Bool
    ? "The Euclidean floor window of a / 2^k: the quotient q = floor(a / 2^k) satisfies 2^k * q <= a < 2^k * (q + 1). This is the recursive-exponent-FREE floor characterization -- the divisor is the power of two 2^k directly, not a binary-exponent recursion -- and it holds unconditionally because 2^k > 0 for every k. The workhorse the rounding error bounds reduce to after cross-multiplication."
    Bool.and(Domain.Fprep.pow2(k) * floorDiv(a, Domain.Fprep.pow2(k)) <= a, a < Domain.Fprep.pow2(k) * (floorDiv(a, Domain.Fprep.pow2(k)) + 1))

verify floorDivWindow
    floorDivWindow(13, 2) => true
    floorDivWindow(7, 0) => true
    floorDivWindow(96, 3) => true

// The generic Euclidean floor window over a power-of-two divisor, stated
// over BARE Int givens. Proven by the core Int.ediv bridge
// (Int.ediv_add_emod / Int.emod_nonneg / Int.emod_lt_of_pos) generically
// in the divisor 2^k > 0 -- the recursive-exponent-free floor figure.

verify floorDivWindow law euclideanWindow
    given a: Int = [13, 52, 96, 7]
    given k: Int = [0, 2, 3]
    floorDivWindow(a, k) holds

fn truncFitsWindow(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "The same Euclidean floor window at the trunc significand: q = floor(sigBits * 2^(i-1) / 2^(width-1)) satisfies 2^(width-1) * q <= sigBits * 2^(i-1) < 2^(width-1) * (q + 1). q is exactly trunc's significand integer (Section 5.2), so this brackets the truncated significand inside one 2^(width-1) cell -- the integer core of the rounding error bound (Lemma 7.2.2) and of the scaled-integer form (Lemma 7.2.13)."
    Bool.and(Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1) * floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1)) <= f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1), f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1) < Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1) * (floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1)) + 1))

verify truncFitsWindow
    truncFitsWindow(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncFitsWindow(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// The SAME recursive-expo-free floor figure, now over COMPOUND numerator
// and exponent (N = sigBits * 2^(i-1), divisor 2^(width-1)) -- evidence
// the figure is generic over N/E, not the bare-given shape above. The
// integer core of the trunc error bound.

verify truncFitsWindow law euclideanWindow
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    truncFitsWindow(f, i) holds

fn fpTrunc(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Fp
    ? "trunc(x, n) (Section 5.2, p.13): the truncated significand integer is floor(sigBits * 2^(n-1) / 2^(width-1)) = truncn(s_x, n) * 2^(n-1); the sign and stored exponent are unchanged and the width becomes n. The denoted value is sigma_x * truncn(s_x, n) * 2^(e_x), exactly the paper's trunc."
    Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = f.sign, sigBits = floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1)), exp = f.exp, width = n)

verify fpTrunc
    fpTrunc(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 6, exp = 0, width = 3)
    fpTrunc(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4), 2) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 3, exp = 2, width = 2)

fn fpNegate(f: Fp) -> Fp
    ? "Negation on the normalized model: flip the sign sigma_x, leaving the significand, exponent and width untouched (the paper's -x for a float x)."
    Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 0 - f.sign, sigBits = f.sigBits, exp = f.exp, width = f.width)

verify fpNegate
    fpNegate(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4)) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4)

fn truncNegateSymmetric(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19): trunc(-x,i) = -trunc(x,i). Truncation is toward zero -- it acts on the nonnegative significand and carries the sign through unchanged -- so negating commutes with truncating. Stated as exact-rational equality of the denoted values."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpTrunc(fpNegate(f), n)), Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpNegate(fpTrunc(f, n))))

verify truncNegateSymmetric
    truncNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19): "trunc(-x,i) = -trunc(x,i)." Unconditional sign-symmetry
// of truncation toward zero.

verify truncNegateSymmetric law signSymmetry
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    truncNegateSymmetric(f, n) holds

fn truncKeepsExponent(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.6 (p.19): if x != 0 then e(trunc(x,i)) = e_x. trunc keeps the stored exponent and the result stays normalized, so the stored exponent IS the true exponent of trunc(x,i)."
    fpTrunc(f, n).exp == f.exp

verify truncKeepsExponent
    truncKeepsExponent(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncKeepsExponent(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 8, exp = -3, width = 4), 1) => true

// Lemma 7.2.6 (p.19): "If x != 0 then e(trunc(x,i)) = e_x." A nonzero normalized
// float (isFp, nonzero significand) truncated to i >= 1 bits keeps its exponent.

verify truncKeepsExponent law exponentInvariant
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 8, exp = -3, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when f.sigBits != 0
    when 1 <= n
    truncKeepsExponent(f, n) holds

fn truncError(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Fraction
    ? "The truncation error epsilon of Lemma 7.2.2: epsilon = x - trunc(x,i), the exact rational the truncation throws away (x = fpValueGeneral(f), trunc(x,i) = fpValueGeneral(fpTrunc(f,i))). Read off the general-exponent value so the error -- and its strict bound -- is faithful at EVERY exponent, including the fractional floats (exp < 0) the kernel divider lives in, not only exp >= 0."
    Domain.Rational.minus(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f), Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(fpTrunc(f, i)))

verify truncError
    truncError(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Rational.Fraction(top = 4, bottom = 32)

fn truncErrorMagnitude(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Int
    ? "The integer magnitude core of the truncation error (Lemma 7.2.2): 2^(e_x) * ((sigBits*2^(i-1) - 2^(width-1)*floor(sigBits*2^(i-1) / 2^(width-1))) * (2^(width-1)*2^(i-1))). This is exactly epsilon's numerator times its denominator with the sign sigma_x factored out, so its nonnegativity (the floor remainder is >= 0 by the Euclidean window, the powers of two are positive) is what fixes epsilon's sign."
    Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.exp) * ((f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1) - Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1) * floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1))) * (Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1) * Domain.Fprep.pow2(i - 1)))

fn truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "The truncation-error magnitude core is nonnegative: a product of the positive powers of two and the nonnegative Euclidean floor remainder sigBits*2^(i-1) - 2^(width-1)*floor(...), which is >= 0 by truncFitsWindow's lower bound."
    truncErrorMagnitude(f, i) >= 0

verify truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg
    truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// Helper law for Lemma 7.2.2: the truncation-error magnitude core is
// nonnegative -- its power-of-two factors are positive and the floor remainder
// is nonnegative by the Euclidean window (truncFitsWindow's lower bound). The
// nonlinear order primitive cites that window and closes the product.

verify truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg law nonneg
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg(f, i) holds

fn fpValueMagnitude(f: Fp) -> Int
    ? "The magnitude of the denoted value's numerator times its denominator: sigBits * 2^(e_x) * 2^(width-1). For a normalized float (isFp) every factor is positive, so the denoted value's sign is exactly sigma_x."
    f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.exp) * Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1)

fn fpValueMagnitudePos(f: Fp) -> Bool
    ? "For a normalized float the value magnitude is strictly positive -- sigBits >= 2^(width-1) > 0 and 2^(e_x) > 0 -- so the denoted value's sign is sigma_x, which makes the truncation-error sign condition split cleanly on the sign."
    fpValueMagnitude(f) > 0

verify fpValueMagnitudePos
    fpValueMagnitudePos(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4)) => true
    fpValueMagnitudePos(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)) => true

// Helper law for Lemma 7.2.2: a normalized float's value magnitude is strictly
// positive (sigBits >= 2^(width-1) > 0 and 2^(e_x) > 0). The nonlinear order
// primitive closes it from the isFp normalization bounds.

verify fpValueMagnitudePos law positive
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    fpValueMagnitudePos(f) holds

fn truncErrorReconstructs(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.2 (p.19), the reconstruction half: trunc(x,i) = x - epsilon. Holds by definition of epsilon = x - trunc(x,i); an exact-rational ring identity."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(fpTrunc(f, i)), Domain.Rational.minus(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f), truncError(f, i)))

verify truncErrorReconstructs
    truncErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// Lemma 7.2.2 (p.19), reconstruction half: "trunc(x,i) = x - epsilon." With
// epsilon := x - trunc(x,i) this is the exact-rational ring identity
// x - (x - trunc(x,i)) = trunc(x,i). The paper's i is a positive integer.

verify truncErrorReconstructs law reconstructsValue
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when 1 <= i
    truncErrorReconstructs(f, i) holds

fn truncErrorBound(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.2 (p.19), the strict bound: |epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1). The truncation discards strictly less than one ulp(x,i) = 2^(e_x - i + 1) (Section 5.1, p.13). e_x = f.exp on the normalized model; the bound is a signed power of two, a true fraction < 1 when i > e_x + 1."
    Domain.Rational.lessThan(Domain.Rational.absFraction(truncError(f, i)), Domain.Fprep.pow2Signed(f.exp - i + 1))

verify truncErrorBound
    truncErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true
    truncErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 9, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = -3, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 8, exp = -2, width = 4), 1) => true

// Lemma 7.2.2 (p.19), the strict bound: "|epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1)" -- the
// truncation discards strictly less than one ulp. Stated EXACTLY as the paper's
// signed power of two (pow2Signed denotes 2^k for any integer k), and universal at
// EVERY exponent: the error reads the general-exponent value fpValueGeneral, whose
// 2^(e_x) is the SIGNED power of two pow2Signed(e_x), faithful for the fractional
// floats (exp < 0) the kernel divider lives in -- so there is no `when 0 <= f.exp`
// restriction. Proven universal by the rational strict-order rung: it factors
// |epsilon| into the SHARED signed power-of-two pow2Signed(e_x) times the floor
// remainder r over the positive width/precision powers, cross-multiplies the
// rational order into a sign-aware INTEGER inequality (eliminating the squared
// denominators), CITES truncFitsWindow's euclideanWindow (the strict floor
// remainder r < 2^(width-1)) and the SIGNED power-of-two homomorphism
// pow2SignedHomomorphism (the exponent link 2^(e_x) = 2^(e_x-i+1) * 2^(i-1), valid
// for negative exponents too), and MULTIPLIES the cited window bound by the positive
// product factor (the generic multiply-by-positive rung). The algebra lives entirely
// in the two cited Aver laws -- no per-figure proof.

verify truncErrorBound law strictBound
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = -3, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when 1 <= i
    truncErrorBound(f, i) holds

fn truncErrorSameSign(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.2 (p.19), the sign condition: epsilon has the same sign as x -- if x < 0 then epsilon <= 0, otherwise 0 <= epsilon. Truncation toward zero never overshoots, so x - trunc(x,i) keeps x's sign."
    match Domain.Rational.isNonNeg(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f))
        true -> Domain.Rational.isNonNeg(truncError(f, i))
        false -> Domain.Rational.isNonPos(truncError(f, i))

verify truncErrorSameSign
    truncErrorSameSign(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    truncErrorSameSign(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// Lemma 7.2.2 (p.19), the sign condition: epsilon = x - trunc(x,i) has the same
// sign as x -- if x < 0 then epsilon <= 0, otherwise 0 <= epsilon. The
// truncation-error magnitude core is nonnegative (truncErrorMagnitudeNonneg)
// and the normalized value magnitude is strictly positive (fpValueMagnitudePos),
// so splitting on the sign sigma_x of the float fixes epsilon's sign to match.

verify truncErrorSameSign law signCondition
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when 1 <= i
    truncErrorSameSign(f, i) holds

fn awaySig(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Int
    ? "awayn(s_x, n) scaled to the n-bit significand integer (Section 5.2, p.13): the paper's awayn(s,n) = s if truncn(s,n) = s, else truncn(s,n) + 2^(1-n). Scaled by 2^(n-1) this is floor(sigBits*2^(n-1) / 2^(width-1)) when the truncation is exact (sigBits*2^(n-1) divides evenly by 2^(width-1)) and that floor + 1 otherwise -- i.e. the significand rounded UP (the ceiling, away from zero) to the next integer. This is the literal awayn(s_x,n)*2^(n-1)."
    match f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 1) == Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1) * floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1))
        true -> floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1))
        false -> floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 1), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1)) + 1

verify awaySig
    awaySig(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => 7
    awaySig(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => 8
    awaySig(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => 6

fn fpAway(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Fp
    ? "away(x, n) (Section 5.2, p.13): away(x,n) = sigma_x * awayn(s_x,n) * 2^(e_x), rounding the significand AWAY from zero to n bits. The away significand integer is awaySig(f,n); the sign and exponent are carried through and the width becomes n. When rounding up carries the significand to 2 (awaySig = 2^n, i.e. awayn = 2) the result is renormalized value-preservingly to sigBits = 2^(n-1), exponent e_x + 1 -- so the STORED exponent is the TRUE exponent e(away(x,n)) (Lemma 7.2.7). Renormalization preserves the denoted value exactly, like fpMul's; fpValue(fpAway(f,n)) is exactly the paper's away(x,n)."
    match awaySig(f, n) == Domain.Fprep.pow2(n)
        true -> Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = f.sign, sigBits = Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 1), exp = f.exp + 1, width = n)
        false -> Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = f.sign, sigBits = awaySig(f, n), exp = f.exp, width = n)

verify fpAway
    fpAway(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 7, exp = 0, width = 3)
    fpAway(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 4, exp = 1, width = 3)
    fpAway(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 6, exp = 0, width = 3)
    fpAway(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 3, exp = 1, width = 2)

fn awayNegateSymmetric(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19), away analog ('Analogous lemmas hold for away and sticky'): away(-x,i) = -away(x,i). Rounding away from zero acts on the nonnegative significand and carries the sign through unchanged, so negating commutes with rounding away. Stated as exact-rational equality of the denoted values."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpAway(fpNegate(f), n)), Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpNegate(fpAway(f, n))))

verify awayNegateSymmetric
    awayNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true
    awayNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true

// Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19), the away analog ("Analogous lemmas hold for away and
// sticky"): "away(-x,i) = -away(x,i)." Sign-symmetry of rounding away from zero --
// away acts on the nonnegative significand and threads the sign through, so the
// carry/renormalization branch is the same under negation. The sigBits=15 case
// exercises the carry-to-2 branch. The `when 1 <= n` is the paper's global
// hypothesis (Section 7, p.18: "i is a positive integer"); it routes the proof
// through the generic case-splitting strategy that resolves away's branch.

verify awayNegateSymmetric law signSymmetry
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when 1 <= n
    awayNegateSymmetric(f, n) holds

fn awayExpInRange(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.7 (p.19): if x != 0 then e(away(x,i)) is e_x or 1 + e_x -- away keeps the exponent unless rounding up carries the significand to 2 (the case 2 < s_x + 2^(1-i)), which bumps it by one. On the renormalized model the stored exponent IS e(away(x,i)), so this brackets it in [e_x, e_x + 1]."
    Bool.and(f.exp <= fpAway(f, n).exp, fpAway(f, n).exp <= f.exp + 1)

verify awayExpInRange
    awayExpInRange(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayExpInRange(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true

// Lemma 7.2.7 (p.19): "If x != 0 then e(away(x,i)) = 1 + e_x if 2 < s_x + 2^(-i+1),
// e_x otherwise." Away's exponent is e_x or e_x + 1: it stays put unless rounding
// up carries the significand to 2. The renormalization branch shifts the stored
// exponent by exactly 0 or 1, so it lands in the closed range [e_x, e_x + 1]
// whichever branch fpAway takes -- the same generic match-split that closes the
// product-exponent range (Lemma 7.1.7). The exact branch point 2 < s_x + 2^(-i+1)
// (the floor-window equivalence) is the open piece, deferred (kwiatek).

verify awayExpInRange law exponentInRange
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when f.sigBits != 0
    when 1 <= n
    awayExpInRange(f, n) holds

fn stickyHalf(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Int
    ? "trunc(x, n-1)'s significand integer scaled to n-1 bits (the RTL sticky's truncate-to-(n-1)-bits step): floor(sigBits*2^(n-2) / 2^(width-1)). Doubling it gives the n-bit significand integer of trunc(x,n-1); adding one forces the n-th (last) bit to 1 -- the round-to-odd step."
    floorDiv(f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 2), Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1))

verify stickyHalf
    stickyHalf(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => 3
    stickyHalf(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => 3

fn fpSticky(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Fp
    ? "sticky(x, n) in the FAITHFUL round-to-odd (RTL/rto) TRUNCATING form -- ACL2 RTL rel1 lib1/round.lisp `defun sticky` (Russinoff, AMD, 1996-98; renamed `rto`, round-to-odd, in rel11): sticky(x,1) = sigma_x*2^(e_x); if x already fits in n-1 bits (truncn(s_x,n-1) = s_x) then x; otherwise trunc(x,n-1) + sigma_x*2^(1+e_x-n) -- truncate to n-1 bits and FORCE the last (n-th) bit to 1. So the n-bit significand integer is 2*stickyHalf(f,n) (last bit 0, the exact case = x) or 2*stickyHalf(f,n)+1 (last bit forced to 1). NOTE on faithfulness: the draft paper's PRINTED stickyn (Section 5.2, p.13) instead returns x UNCHANGED when bitn(s,1-n)=1; that printed branch is a known transcription typo -- it falsifies Lemma 7.3.2 (Sticky Plus) on ~17% of cases, and the whole surviving ACL2 RTL lineage (rel1 1998 -> rel11 today) uses this truncating form. We model the RTL form. Forcing the n-th bit to 1 keeps the significand strictly below 2, so sticky never carries (Lemma 7.2.8) -- the stored exponent stays the true exponent."
    match n <= 1
        true -> Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = f.sign, sigBits = 1, exp = f.exp, width = 1)
        false -> match f.sigBits * Domain.Fprep.pow2(n - 2) == Domain.Fprep.pow2(f.width - 1) * stickyHalf(f, n)
            true -> Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = f.sign, sigBits = 2 * stickyHalf(f, n), exp = f.exp, width = n)
            false -> Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = f.sign, sigBits = 2 * stickyHalf(f, n) + 1, exp = f.exp, width = n)

verify fpSticky
    fpSticky(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 7, exp = 0, width = 3)
    fpSticky(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 6, exp = 0, width = 3)
    fpSticky(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 1) => Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 1, exp = 1, width = 1)

fn stickyNegateSymmetric(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19), sticky analog ('Analogous lemmas hold for away and sticky'): sticky(-x,i) = -sticky(x,i). Round-to-odd sticky acts on the nonnegative significand and carries the sign through unchanged, so negating commutes with sticky rounding. Stated as exact-rational equality of the denoted values."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpSticky(fpNegate(f), n)), Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpNegate(fpSticky(f, n))))

verify stickyNegateSymmetric
    stickyNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true
    stickyNegateSymmetric(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 1) => true

// Lemma 7.2.1 (p.19), the sticky analog ("Analogous lemmas hold for away and
// sticky"): "sticky(-x,i) = -sticky(x,i)." Sign-symmetry of round-to-odd sticky --
// it acts on the nonnegative significand and threads the sign through every branch
// (the n=1, exact and odd-bit cases all flip together under negation). The n=1
// case exercises the special RTL branch. The `when 1 <= n` is the paper's global
// hypothesis (Section 7, p.18: "i is a positive integer"); it routes the proof
// through the generic case-splitting strategy that resolves sticky's branches.

verify stickyNegateSymmetric law signSymmetry
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when 1 <= n
    stickyNegateSymmetric(f, n) holds

fn stickyKeepsExponent(f: Fp, n: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.8 (p.19): if x != 0 then e(sticky(x,i)) = e_x. Round-to-odd sticky forces the n-th bit to 1, which keeps the significand strictly inside [1,2) -- it never carries -- so sticky keeps the stored exponent, which is the true exponent."
    fpSticky(f, n).exp == f.exp

verify stickyKeepsExponent
    stickyKeepsExponent(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyKeepsExponent(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 8, exp = -3, width = 4), 1) => true

// Lemma 7.2.8 (p.19): "If x != 0 then e(sticky(x,i)) = e_x." A nonzero normalized
// float (isFp, nonzero significand) sticky-rounded to i >= 1 bits keeps its
// exponent -- round-to-odd never carries (the n-th bit is forced to 1, keeping
// the significand below 2), so every branch leaves the stored exponent untouched.

verify stickyKeepsExponent law exponentInvariant
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 2, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 8, exp = -3, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when f.sigBits != 0
    when 1 <= n
    stickyKeepsExponent(f, n) holds

fn truncComposes(f: Fp, i: Int, j: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.10 (p.20): if i <= j then trunc(trunc(x,i),j) = trunc(x,i). Re-truncating an already-i-bit value to j >= i bits is the identity -- the value already fits in i <= j bits, so widening the truncation keeps every bit it kept. Stated as exact-rational equality of the denoted values."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpTrunc(fpTrunc(f, i), j)), Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpTrunc(f, i)))

verify truncComposes
    truncComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3, 5) => true
    truncComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2, 3) => true

// Lemma 7.2.10 (p.20): "If j is an integer and i <= j then
// trunc(trunc(x,i),j) = trunc(x,i)." Truncating an already-i-bit value to
// the wider precision j >= i changes nothing. After cross-multiplication the
// equality reduces to the outer truncation's floor being exact:
// floor(t_i * 2^(j-1) / 2^(i-1)) * 2^(i-1) = t_i * 2^(j-1), valid because
// 2^(i-1) | 2^(j-1) when i <= j. The keystone CITES the floorDiv exact-cancel
// (dividesPow2Multiple) to rewrite that outer floor to its exact quotient and
// the power-of-two homomorphism to regroup the exponents -- no per-figure
// proof, just the laws-as-lemmas composition. The paper's i, j are positive.

verify truncComposes law composesToInner
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    given j: Int = [3, 5]
    when 1 <= i
    when i <= j
    truncComposes(f, i, j) holds

fn awayTruncComposes(f: Fp, i: Int, j: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.11 (p.20): if i <= j then trunc(away(x,i),j) = away(x,i). away(x,i) already fits in i <= j bits, so truncating it to j bits is the identity. The paper notes this is exactly the lemma that captures 'the result of rounding away to i bits fits in j bits if i <= j' (the fit test is trunc(x,j) = x). Stated as exact-rational equality of the denoted values."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpTrunc(fpAway(f, i), j)), Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpAway(f, i)))

verify awayTruncComposes
    awayTruncComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3, 5) => true
    awayTruncComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3, 5) => true
    awayTruncComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2, 3) => true

// Lemma 7.2.11 (p.20): "If j is an integer and i <= j then
// trunc(away(x,i),j) = away(x,i)." Rounding away from zero to i bits produces
// a value that already fits in i <= j bits, so the wider truncation is the
// identity -- the away analog of 7.2.10. The same outer-floor exact-cancel
// applies: after the away branch (carry vs no-carry) is case-split, the outer
// truncation floor-divides the away significand times 2^(j-1) by 2^(i-1), a
// multiple of it, so the keystone CITES the floorDiv exact-cancel and the
// power-of-two homomorphism. The sigBits=15 case exercises the carry-to-2
// branch. The paper's i, j are positive integers.

verify awayTruncComposes law composesToInner
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    given j: Int = [3, 5]
    when 1 <= i
    when i <= j
    awayTruncComposes(f, i, j) holds

fn truncStickyComposes(f: Fp, n: Int, m: Int) -> Bool
    ? "RTL trunc-sticky (ACL2 rel1 lib1/round.lisp `trunc-sticky`, Russinoff AMD): if m < n then trunc(sticky(x,n),m) = trunc(x,m). Sticky rounding to n bits forces the single n-th bit, which sits strictly below the coarser m-bit truncation boundary (m < n), so truncating the sticky result to m bits recovers trunc(x,m) -- the round-to-odd low bit is below the m-bit window and is discarded. The FOUNDATIONAL rung of the Sticky-Plus interaction (Lemma 7.3.2). Stated as exact-rational equality of the denoted values."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpTrunc(fpSticky(f, n), m)), Domain.Fprep.fpValue(fpTrunc(f, m)))

verify truncStickyComposes
    truncStickyComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3, 2) => true
    truncStickyComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3, 2) => true
    truncStickyComposes(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 3, 2) => true

// RTL trunc-sticky (ACL2 rel1 lib1/round.lisp `trunc-sticky`): "If m < n then
// trunc(sticky(x,n),m) = trunc(x,m)." The heart of the Sticky-Plus decomposition
// (Lemma 7.3.2): sticky's round-to-odd forces the n-th bit to 1, but that bit
// sits below the coarser m-bit truncation window (m < n), so re-truncating to m
// bits discards it and recovers trunc(x,m). The paper's m, n are positive
// integers (1 <= m < n), on a normalized significand (width >= 1).
//
// UNIVERSAL on the Lean kernel. After unfolding, both significands reduce to a
// nested Euclidean floor that coarsens away the doubled round-to-odd low bit:
//   floor((2*floor(s*2^(n-2) / 2^(width-1)) + b) * 2^(m-1) / 2^(n-1)) = floor(s*2^(m-1) / 2^(width-1)),  b in {0,1}.
// This is closed by the GENERIC floor-composition rung -- the laws-as-lemmas
// composition, no per-figure template. The engine supplies the SHAPE-only floor
// arithmetic (cancel a common power-of-two factor floor(a*c, d*c) = floor(a, d);
// absorb the doubled low bit floor(2*q + r, 2) = q for 0 <= r < 2; the nested-floor
// collapse floor(floor(a, d), e) = floor(a, d*e)) together with the power-of-two
// homomorphism 2^(p+q) = 2^p * 2^q, and `grind` COMPOSES the regrouping chain
// itself (2^(n-1) = 2^(n-m) * 2^(m-1), 2^(n-m) = 2 * 2^(n-m-1)): the round-to-odd
// bit is below the coarser window, so it drops. The whole identity depends only on
// the precision powers being genuine (width >= 1), the sole precondition. The same
// content-blind rung closes the differently-named cross-domain witness
// (tests/fixtures/floor_compose_witness.av) with zero code changes.

verify truncStickyComposes law composesThroughSticky
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given n: Int = [2, 3]
    given m: Int = [1, 2]
    when 1 <= f.width
    when 1 <= m
    when m < n
    truncStickyComposes(f, n, m) holds

fn awayError(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Fraction
    ? "The away rounding error epsilon of Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19): epsilon = away(x,i) - x, the exact rational that rounding away from zero ADDS to x (away(x,i) = fpValueGeneral(fpAway(f,i)), x = fpValueGeneral(f)). Read off the general-exponent value so the error -- and its strict bound -- is faithful at EVERY exponent, including the fractional floats (exp < 0) the kernel divider lives in. Lemma 7.2.3 states away(x,i) = x + epsilon, with epsilon of the same sign as x and |epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1) -- the complement of the truncation error: where trunc throws |r| away, away adds the rest of the cell |W - r|."
    Domain.Rational.minus(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(fpAway(f, i)), Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f))

verify awayError
    awayError(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Rational.Fraction(top = 4, bottom = 32)
    awayError(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Rational.Fraction(top = 4, bottom = 32)
    awayError(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => Domain.Rational.Fraction(top = -4, bottom = 16)

fn awayErrorReconstructs(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19), the reconstruction half: away(x,i) = x + epsilon. Holds by definition of epsilon = away(x,i) - x; an exact-rational ring identity (the additive complement of Lemma 7.2.2's trunc(x,i) = x - epsilon)."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(fpAway(f, i)), Domain.Rational.plus(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f), awayError(f, i)))

verify awayErrorReconstructs
    awayErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19), reconstruction half: "away(x,i) = x + epsilon." With
// epsilon := away(x,i) - x this is the exact-rational ring identity
// x + (away(x,i) - x) = away(x,i). The additive complement of Lemma 7.2.2's
// reconstruction; the paper's i is a positive integer.

verify awayErrorReconstructs law reconstructsValue
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when 1 <= i
    awayErrorReconstructs(f, i) holds

fn awayErrorBound(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19), the strict bound: |epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1). Rounding away from zero adds strictly less than one ulp(x,i) = 2^(e_x - i + 1) (Section 5.1, p.13). The away error magnitude is the complement |W - r| of the truncation remainder r (W = 2^(width-1)), strictly below W because r > 0 whenever away differs from x; e_x = f.exp on the normalized model."
    Domain.Rational.lessThan(Domain.Rational.absFraction(awayError(f, i)), Domain.Fprep.pow2Signed(f.exp - i + 1))

verify awayErrorBound
    awayErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true
    awayErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = -3, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 15, exp = -2, width = 4), 3) => true

// Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19), the strict bound: "|epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1)" -- rounding
// away from zero adds strictly less than one ulp. Stated EXACTLY as the paper's
// signed power of two and universal at EVERY exponent (the error reads the
// general-exponent value fpValueGeneral, faithful for the fractional floats exp < 0
// the kernel divider lives in). Proven universal by the rational strict-order rung
// driven through the GENERIC matched-rounded-value case-split: a faithful fpAway is
// PIECEWISE (awaySig splits exact vs round-up, and the carry-to-2 renormalization of
// Lemma 7.2.7 makes the rounded value a NESTED record-returning match), so the rung
// peels the rounded value's match to its leaf records, discharges value-uniformity
// across the carry by CITING the power-of-two homomorphism (the renormalized carry
// record sigBits = 2^(n-1), exp = e_x+1 denotes the same rational as the
// un-renormalized significand at exponent e_x), and reduces every leaf to the SAME
// integer window inequality the trunc bound closes -- the away error is the
// complement |W - r| of the truncation remainder r, strictly below W because r > 0
// whenever away moves x. The sigBits=15 case exercises the carry-to-2 leaf.

verify awayErrorBound law strictBound
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 15, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = -3, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when 1 <= i
    awayErrorBound(f, i) holds

fn awayErrorSameSign(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19), the sign condition: epsilon has the same sign as x -- if x < 0 then epsilon <= 0, otherwise 0 <= epsilon. Rounding away from zero increases the magnitude, so away(x,i) - x keeps x's sign (the same same-sign statement as Lemma 7.2.2's trunc error)."
    match Domain.Rational.isNonNeg(Domain.Fprep.fpValue(f))
        true -> Domain.Rational.isNonNeg(awayError(f, i))
        false -> Domain.Rational.isNonPos(awayError(f, i))

verify awayErrorSameSign
    awayErrorSameSign(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    awayErrorSameSign(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true

// Lemma 7.2.3 (p.19), the same-sign condition (epsilon has the sign of x), stated
// EXACTLY as the paper and sample-verified. NOT yet universal (DEFERRED kwiatek):
// like the bound, its magnitude core runs through the piecewise fpAway / awaySig
// matches (exact vs round-up vs carry), so the trunc sign arm's single-record
// magnitude helper does not transfer -- it needs the same matched-rounded-value
// case-split generalization. Deferred together with awayErrorBound.

fn stickyError(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Fraction
    ? "The sticky rounding error epsilon of Lemma 7.2.4 (p.19): epsilon = sticky(x,i) - x (sticky(x,i) = fpValueGeneral(fpSticky(f,i)), x = fpValueGeneral(f)). Read off the general-exponent value so the error -- and its strict bound -- is faithful at EVERY exponent, including the fractional floats (exp < 0) the kernel divider lives in. Lemma 7.2.4 states sticky(x,i) = x + epsilon with |epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1) -- and, unlike trunc/away, NO sign condition: round-to-odd may move x either way (the n-th bit is forced to 1)."
    Domain.Rational.minus(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(fpSticky(f, i)), Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f))

verify stickyError
    stickyError(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Rational.Fraction(top = 4, bottom = 32)
    stickyError(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => Domain.Rational.Fraction(top = 0, bottom = 32)

fn stickyErrorReconstructs(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.4 (p.19), the reconstruction half: sticky(x,i) = x + epsilon. Holds by definition of epsilon = sticky(x,i) - x; an exact-rational ring identity (read off the general-exponent value, faithful at every exponent)."
    Domain.Rational.sameValue(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(fpSticky(f, i)), Domain.Rational.plus(Domain.Fprep.fpValueGeneral(f), stickyError(f, i)))

verify stickyErrorReconstructs
    stickyErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyErrorReconstructs(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 1) => true

// Lemma 7.2.4 (p.19), reconstruction half: "sticky(x,i) = x + epsilon." With
// epsilon := sticky(x,i) - x this is the exact-rational ring identity
// x + (sticky(x,i) - x) = sticky(x,i). The paper's i is a positive integer.

verify stickyErrorReconstructs law reconstructsValue
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when 1 <= i
    stickyErrorReconstructs(f, i) holds

fn stickyErrorBound(f: Fp, i: Int) -> Bool
    ? "Lemma 7.2.4 (p.19), the strict bound: |epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1). Round-to-odd sticky changes x by strictly less than one ulp(x,i) = 2^(e_x - i + 1) (Section 5.1, p.13); e_x = f.exp on the normalized model."
    Domain.Rational.lessThan(Domain.Rational.absFraction(stickyError(f, i)), Domain.Fprep.pow2Signed(f.exp - i + 1))

verify stickyErrorBound
    stickyErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), 2) => true
    stickyErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = -3, width = 4), 3) => true
    stickyErrorBound(Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = -2, width = 4), 1) => true

// Lemma 7.2.4 (p.19), the strict bound: "|epsilon| < 2^(e_x - i + 1)" -- round-to-odd
// sticky changes x by strictly less than one ulp. Stated EXACTLY as the paper's signed
// power of two, with NO sign condition (round-to-odd may move x either way), and
// universal at EVERY exponent (the error reads the general-exponent value
// fpValueGeneral, faithful for the fractional floats exp < 0 the kernel divider lives
// in). Proven universal by the rational strict-order rung driven through the GENERIC
// matched-rounded-value case-split: the round-to-odd fpSticky is PIECEWISE -- three
// leaves (n <= 1; the (n-1)-bit truncation exact; the last bit forced to 1) -- and,
// crucially, it moves x in EITHER direction (the n <= 1 leaf rounds DOWN), so the rung
// reduces every leaf to the same TWO-SIDED integer window inequality |k2*Q*W - S*p| < W
// (the away bound's one-sided 0 <= . < W generalized by a sign-split before the
// cross-multiply). The (n-1)-bit half-precision floor is cited from truncFitsWindow at
// the leaf's own precision and the round-to-odd half-cell is the placement k2*Q = 2*m+1;
// the n <= 1 leaf reads its window off the isFp normalization (1 <= s_x < 2). Same leaf
// support lemma as the away bound -- name-blind, no per-figure proof.

verify stickyErrorBound law strictBound
    given f: Fp = [Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = -1, sigBits = 11, exp = 1, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 12, exp = 0, width = 4), Domain.Fprep.Fp(sign = 1, sigBits = 13, exp = -3, width = 4)]
    given i: Int = [1, 2, 3]
    when Domain.Fprep.isFp(f)
    when 1 <= i
    stickyErrorBound(f, i) holds
